# Line endings, pinned so the repo behaves the same on every machine.
#
# Copied from `StopSleepApp` on 2026-08-22 for the same reason and with the same effect.
#
# Why it exists: every single `git add` printed
#   warning: in the working copy of '<file>', LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it
# for every text file it touched. That is not a defect in the files — it is git's global
# `core.autocrlf=true` on this machine announcing that it WOULD convert them, because nothing in
# the repository said what the line endings should be. With dozens of files per commit the warning
# buried real output.
#
# ⚠️ The fix is to state it here rather than to change `core.autocrlf`, and the difference matters:
# a global git setting is a property of THIS MACHINE and travels with nobody. `.gitattributes` is
# committed, so a second contributor — or a CI checkout — gets the same answer without configuring
# anything.
#
# LF is chosen, not inherited: the index already stores LF for all 81 text files, so this pins what
# is true today and makes the working tree match. Nothing is renormalised and no diff is produced.
#
# ⚠️ `gradlew.bat` does not exist here — this is a static site — but the `*.bat` rule is kept so the
# three repos answer the line-ending question identically rather than each in its own way.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the one exception — cmd.exe requires CRLF, and `gradlew.bat` is executed
# directly by the build. Left as LF it fails in ways that do not name line endings.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Binaries: marked explicitly so git never inspects or converts them. `binary` is shorthand for
# `-text -diff`, which also stops them being rendered as garbage in a diff.
*.png binary
*.webp binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.jar binary
*.keystore binary
*.jks binary
*.apk binary
*.aab binary
*.so binary
